Fundamentals of Materials Science — MIT 3.012 (@mat-mit-fundamentals: 0, is #reference, #part-of @mat-sources) { r_1JE4A3P3KKM0FZXERWFATXCS1Z
Darrell Irvine and Nicola Marzari, MIT 3.012, Fall 2005. [Course](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-012-fundamentals-of-materials-science-fall-2005/), [lecture notes](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-012-fundamentals-of-materials-science-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/), [assignments](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-012-fundamentals-of-materials-science-fall-2005/pages/assignments/) and [examinations](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-012-fundamentals-of-materials-science-fall-2005/pages/exams/).

Work both structure/bonding and thermodynamics sequences progressively, including substantial exercises. The opening materials section uses structure, symmetry, bonding, disordered materials and diffraction; later work develops thermodynamic stability and transformations. Use the annotated versions where available and fuller linked textbooks when slides omit steps.

The course index, orientation and selected structure/bonding lecture PDFs 16 and 21, plus thermodynamics lecture 17 were inspected on 30 October 2026. They explicitly remove some copyrighted illustrations and refer to separately published books; public slides are not a complete copy of those books. Cambridge's fuller illustrated lessons and the reused mineralogy/crystallography texts support those gaps. OCW's CC BY-NC-SA terms have component-specific exceptions. Source media, assignments and software remain external.

**Return:** [Materials-engineering sources](../Sources.lingua#r_51JAH6DB4WV2HZAB69G21ZHSMK).

**Eighth chemistry-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** rechecked the official course identity and selected actual text of [thermodynamics lecture 17](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-012-fundamentals-of-materials-science-fall-2005/e4abb04acc7a7b31d1c0964d8caa225f_lec17t.pdf): printed pp. 3–7 on common tangents and the lever rule, pp. 9–10 on binary diagrams, and pp. 11–13 on limited miscibility and the regular-solution discussion. The title says Lecture 17, 11.07.05, while the footers say Lecture 18, 11/6/05; retain the linked file locator rather than silently renumbering it. Extracted text omits some diagram formulas; this is not a fresh complete figure or course audit. The chemistry block adds its own derivations and model checks and reuses the existing materials lessons/program. Existing OCW rights and component exceptions remain; broader lecture/problem assignments and source media stay external.

